VALUES

-- Q1: medium / L2 — Self-rental rule recharacterization
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Thornton owns a medical practice organized as an S corporation and rents an office building she personally owns to the practice for $48,000 per year. The rental activity generates $48,000 of net income. Thornton materially participates in the medical practice. Under the self-rental rule, how is the $48,000 of rental income classified?', '["Passive income that can absorb passive losses from other activities", "Portfolio income reported on Schedule B", "Nonpassive income that cannot offset passive losses from other activities", "Tax-exempt income excluded from gross income"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.469-2(f)(6), rental income from property rented to a business in which the taxpayer materially participates is recharacterized as nonpassive income. This self-rental rule prevents taxpayers from generating passive income to shelter passive losses from other activities. Unlike the first choice suggests, the income cannot absorb passive losses because it is reclassified as nonpassive.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Publicly traded partnership passive loss basket
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Rivera owns interests in two publicly traded partnerships (PTPs). PTP Alpha generates $12,000 of passive income in 2026, and PTP Beta generates $18,000 of passive losses. Rivera also has $25,000 of passive income from a private real estate limited partnership. How are Rivera''s PTP losses treated?', '["The $18,000 PTP Beta loss offsets the $12,000 PTP Alpha income, and $6,000 offsets the private partnership income", "The $18,000 PTP Beta loss can only offset PTP Beta income; the full $18,000 is suspended and carried forward", "The $18,000 PTP Beta loss offsets the $12,000 PTP Alpha income plus $6,000 of private partnership income", "The $18,000 PTP Beta loss is permanently disallowed because PTPs cannot generate deductible losses"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 469(k), passive losses from a publicly traded partnership can only offset passive income from that same PTP. Because PTP Beta generated no income of its own, the entire $18,000 loss is suspended and carried forward to future years. Unlike the first choice suggests, PTP losses cannot cross over to offset income from a different PTP or from private partnerships.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: hard / L4 — Installment sale and suspended passive loss release timing
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'A tax advisor is counseling Patel, who has $150,000 of suspended passive losses from a rental apartment complex. Patel sells the property to an unrelated buyer for $600,000 (adjusted basis $500,000) using a two-year installment note, receiving $200,000 in Year 1 and $400,000 in Year 2. Patel''s marginal rate is 24% in Year 1 and is expected to rise to 37% in Year 2. Evaluate the optimal reporting strategy for the suspended losses.', '["Elect out of installment treatment to recognize all gain and release all suspended losses in Year 1 at the lower 24% rate, avoiding higher-taxed gain in Year 2", "Report on installment method because suspended losses are released proportionally as gain is recognized each year", "Report on installment method because the full $150,000 of suspended losses offsets Year 1 installment gain, and any remainder carries to Year 2", "Elect out because installment sales of passive activities are not considered complete dispositions and losses remain suspended until final payment"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 469(g)(1), a complete disposition in a fully taxable transaction releases all suspended passive losses in the year of disposition regardless of installment reporting. Since all $150,000 of losses are released in Year 1 either way, electing out concentrates the full $100,000 gain in Year 1 at 24% rather than deferring $66,667 to Year 2 at 37%, producing greater net tax savings. Unlike the second choice suggests, suspended losses are not released proportionally—they are fully released upon disposition.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Active participation standard for rental allowance
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'What level of involvement does Section 469(i) require for a taxpayer to claim the $25,000 rental real estate loss allowance?', '["Material participation requiring over 500 hours of service", "Active participation, meaning the taxpayer makes management decisions in a bona fide sense", "No participation requirement beyond property ownership", "Full-time employment in the rental activity exceeding 1,000 hours"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 469(i) allows individuals who actively participate in rental real estate to deduct up to $25,000 of rental losses against nonpassive income. Active participation is a lower standard than material participation—it requires only bona fide management decisions such as approving tenants or setting rental terms. Unlike the first choice suggests, the 500-hour material participation threshold is not required for the rental loss allowance.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: hard / L4 — Evaluate grouping election strategy for real estate professional
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Kowalski, a licensed real estate agent who logs 1,800 hours in real property trades, owns four rental properties. She spends 300 hours on Property A, 250 on Property B, 150 on Property C, and 100 on Property D. Properties A and B generate $80,000 combined losses; Properties C and D generate $60,000 combined losses. Without grouping, she cannot materially participate in C and D individually. Her CPA recommends electing under Reg. Section 1.469-9(g) to treat all four as one activity. Evaluate this recommendation.', '["The election is unnecessary because real estate professionals automatically treat all rentals as nonpassive", "The election is inadvisable because it permanently locks all properties into one activity, preventing selective disposition", "The election is advisable because aggregating yields 800 total hours, satisfying material participation and converting all $140,000 of losses to nonpassive", "The election is inadvisable because 800 aggregate hours do not satisfy any material participation test"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Reg. Section 1.469-9(g), a qualifying real estate professional may elect to treat all rental interests as a single activity. By aggregating, Kowalski''s 800 combined hours exceed the 750-hour material participation threshold under Reg. Section 1.469-5T(a)(1), converting all $140,000 of losses to nonpassive. Without the election, $60,000 from Properties C and D would remain passive. Unlike the first choice suggests, real estate professional status alone does not make rentals nonpassive—material participation must still be established.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Short-term rental excluded from rental activity definition
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Brennan owns a beachfront condo rented to short-term vacationers through an online platform. The average rental period during 2026 is 4.5 days. Brennan spends 600 hours managing bookings, cleaning, and maintaining the property. She has $35,000 of passive losses from a separate limited partnership. How should the condo income be classified under Section 469?', '["Nonpassive business income, because the average rental period is under 7 days and Brennan materially participates", "Passive rental income eligible for the $25,000 special allowance", "Portfolio income because short-term rental proceeds are classified as investment returns", "Passive income that can absorb the $35,000 of losses from the limited partnership"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Reg. Section 1.469-1T(e)(3)(ii)(A), a rental activity with an average customer use period of 7 days or less is not treated as a rental activity for Section 469 purposes. Because Brennan spends 600 hours (exceeding 500), she materially participates, making the income nonpassive. Unlike the fourth choice suggests, the income cannot offset passive losses because it is classified as nonpassive business income.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — At-risk recapture on distribution reducing amount below zero
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Foster invested $90,000 cash in a business activity and deducted $75,000 of losses in prior years, reducing his at-risk amount to $15,000. In 2026, Foster receives a $30,000 cash distribution from the activity. How do the at-risk rules apply to this distribution?', '["The distribution has no effect on the at-risk amount because distributions are return of capital", "The $30,000 reduces at-risk to negative $15,000, but no recapture occurs until the activity is sold", "Only $15,000 reduces the at-risk amount to zero, and the remaining $15,000 is capital gain", "The distribution reduces the at-risk amount below zero, triggering $15,000 of income recapture under Section 465(e)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 465(e), if distributions or other events reduce a taxpayer''s at-risk amount below zero, the taxpayer must recapture previously deducted losses as income. Foster''s $15,000 at-risk amount minus the $30,000 distribution yields negative $15,000, requiring $15,000 of income recapture. Unlike the second choice suggests, recapture is triggered immediately when the at-risk amount goes negative—not deferred until disposition.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Rental and nonrental activity grouping prohibition
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Hartley owns a restaurant and a separate commercial building that she rents to unrelated tenants. She wants to group the restaurant and the rental building as a single activity under Section 469 to aggregate her participation hours. Under the grouping regulations, can Hartley combine these two activities?', '["Yes, because both activities share common ownership and are in the same geographic area", "No, because rental activities generally cannot be grouped with nonrental trade or business activities under Reg. Section 1.469-4(d)(1)", "Yes, but only if the rental income constitutes less than 20% of total combined gross income", "No, because grouping elections are only available to real estate professionals"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Reg. Section 1.469-4(d)(1), a rental activity generally may not be grouped with a trade or business activity. Exceptions exist only when one activity is insubstantial relative to the other, but the general prohibition prevents Hartley from freely combining rental and nonrental activities. Unlike the first choice suggests, common ownership and geographic proximity alone do not override the regulatory prohibition.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Working interest in oil and gas exception
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Garza holds a working interest in an oil and gas drilling operation through a general partnership. Garza does not materially participate in the operation. The activity generates a $45,000 loss in 2026. Under the passive activity rules, how is Garza''s loss classified?', '["Passive loss because Garza does not materially participate", "Portfolio loss deductible only against portfolio income", "Nonpassive loss because working interests in oil and gas held through non-liability-limiting entities are exempt from passive activity rules", "Passive loss limited to Garza''s at-risk amount in the partnership"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 469(c)(3), a working interest in an oil or gas property held through an entity that does not limit the taxpayer''s liability (such as a general partnership) is not treated as a passive activity regardless of participation level. The loss is nonpassive. Unlike the first choice suggests, the lack of material participation does not make this a passive activity because the oil and gas working interest exception overrides the general rule.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — Portfolio income exclusion from passive income
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'What types of income does Section 469(e)(1) exclude from classification as passive activity income?', '["Interest, dividends, annuities, and royalties not derived in the ordinary course of a trade or business", "Rental income from real property held for investment", "Income from any activity in which the taxpayer works fewer than 100 hours", "Self-employment income from a sole proprietorship"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 469(e)(1)(A) specifically excludes portfolio income—including interest, dividends, annuities, and royalties not derived in the ordinary course of business—from passive activity income. This prevents taxpayers from using portfolio income to offset passive losses. Unlike the second choice suggests, rental income is generally classified as passive income, not portfolio income, under the Section 469 rules.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Personal service corporation passive loss restriction
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Whitfield Medical Associates, P.C. is a personal service corporation owned entirely by physicians. The corporation has $200,000 of active medical practice income and $85,000 of passive losses from a real estate investment. Under Section 469, how are the passive losses treated?', '["The passive losses offset the active income because corporations are exempt from passive activity rules", "The passive losses offset up to $25,000 of active income under the rental loss allowance", "The passive losses are permanently disallowed for personal service corporations", "The passive losses can only offset passive income, not the active medical practice income"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 469(a)(2), personal service corporations may only deduct passive activity losses against passive activity income. Unlike closely held C corporations (which may offset passive losses against active business income under Section 469(e)(2)), personal service corporations face the same restriction as individuals. Unlike the first choice suggests, personal service corporations are specifically subject to the passive activity loss rules.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: easy / L2 — Significant participation activity 500-hour aggregate test
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Watts participates in two business activities during 2026. She works 280 hours in Activity P and 240 hours in Activity Q. Neither activity has any other participants. Under the significant participation activity rules, what is the result?', '["Both activities are passive because neither exceeds the 500-hour material participation threshold individually", "Both activities are nonpassive because her aggregate participation of 520 hours across significant participation activities exceeds 500 hours", "Only Activity P is nonpassive because 280 hours exceeds the 250-hour minimum", "Both activities are passive because each must independently meet the 500-hour test"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Reg. Section 1.469-5T(a)(4), a taxpayer materially participates in each significant participation activity (those with more than 100 hours) if aggregate participation across all such activities exceeds 500 hours. Watts''s combined 520 hours exceeds 500, so both activities are treated as nonpassive. Unlike the first choice suggests, the SPA test evaluates aggregate hours, not individual activity hours.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — Related party borrowing excluded from at-risk
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Under Section 465(b)(3), which type of borrowed amount is excluded from a taxpayer''s at-risk amount?', '["Recourse loans from commercial banks", "Qualified nonrecourse financing secured by real property", "Amounts borrowed from a person who has an ownership interest in the activity other than as a creditor", "Cash contributions made directly by the taxpayer"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 465(b)(3) excludes from the at-risk amount any borrowed funds where the lender has an interest in the activity (other than as a creditor) or is related to a person with such an interest. This prevents artificial inflation of at-risk amounts through related-party lending. Unlike the second choice suggests, qualified nonrecourse financing on real estate is a specific exception that increases the at-risk amount under Section 465(b)(6).', 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: easy / L2 — Carryforward of suspended passive losses
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Dunn has $42,000 of suspended passive losses from a limited partnership at the end of 2025. In 2026, the partnership generates $15,000 of passive income. Dunn has no other passive activities. How much of the suspended loss can Dunn use in 2026?', '["$15,000, with the remaining $27,000 carried forward to 2027", "$42,000, because all prior-year suspended losses are deductible in the following year", "$0, because suspended losses from limited partnerships are permanently disallowed", "$25,000, under the special rental loss allowance"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 469(b), disallowed passive losses are carried forward indefinitely and may offset passive income in future years. Dunn can use $15,000 of the $42,000 suspended loss against the 2026 passive income, with the remaining $27,000 carrying forward. Unlike the second choice suggests, suspended losses are only deductible to the extent of current passive income unless a full disposition occurs.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Significant participation activity income recharacterization
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Palmer participates in three business activities for 180, 130, and 110 hours respectively during 2026. Each activity generates net income totaling $40,000 combined. Palmer also has $40,000 of passive losses from a rental property. Her aggregate SPA hours are 420, below the 500-hour threshold. Palmer expects the SPA income to fully offset her rental losses. Is this treatment correct?', '["Yes, because all three activities are passive and the income offsets the passive losses", "Yes, because significant participation activity income is always classified as passive", "No, because the $40,000 is portfolio income that cannot offset passive losses", "No, because under Reg. Section 1.469-2T(f)(2), net income from significant participation activities is recharacterized as nonpassive"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Reg. Section 1.469-2T(f)(2), net income from significant participation activities (activities with 100+ hours but without material participation) is recharacterized as nonpassive income. This prevents taxpayers from generating passive income through limited involvement to shelter passive losses from other activities. Unlike the first choice suggests, SPA income is not treated as passive—the recharacterization rule converts it to nonpassive.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Limited partner material participation restrictions
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Edwards is a limited partner in a technology development partnership. During 2026, Edwards works 480 hours providing consulting services to the partnership but does not meet any other material participation test. Under Section 469(h)(5), does Edwards materially participate?', '["Yes, because 480 hours exceeds the 100-hour threshold for limited partners", "No, because limited partners can only satisfy material participation through the 500-hour, 5-of-10-years, or personal service 3-year tests", "Yes, because any participation exceeding 250 hours satisfies material participation for limited partners", "No, because limited partners are categorically prohibited from ever materially participating"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 469(h)(5) and Reg. Section 1.469-5T(e), a limited partner can establish material participation through only three tests: exceeding 500 hours, materially participating in any 5 of the prior 10 tax years, or materially participating in a personal service activity for any 3 preceding years. Edwards''s 480 hours falls short of 500. Unlike the fourth choice suggests, limited partners are not entirely prohibited—they simply have fewer qualifying tests available.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: easy / L2 — Active participation vs material participation distinction
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Knox owns a rental property and makes decisions about tenant selection and lease terms, spending approximately 30 hours during 2026. Knox''s AGI is $95,000 and the rental generates a $20,000 loss. How is the rental loss treated?', '["Not deductible because 30 hours is insufficient participation", "Fully deductible as a nonpassive loss because Knox materially participates", "Deductible in full under the Section 469(i) special allowance because Knox actively participates and AGI is below the phase-out threshold", "Deductible but limited to $15,000 due to the phase-out starting at $90,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 469(i), active participation requires only bona fide management involvement such as tenant selection—no minimum hour requirement applies. Since Knox''s AGI of $95,000 is below the $100,000 phase-out start, the full $20,000 rental loss is deductible against nonpassive income. Unlike the fourth choice suggests, the phase-out does not begin until AGI exceeds $100,000, not $90,000.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Section 1031 exchange does not release suspended losses
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Tran owns a rental property with $55,000 of suspended passive losses. She exchanges the property for a like-kind replacement under Section 1031, recognizing no gain. Tran continues to rent the replacement property. What happens to the $55,000 of suspended passive losses?', '["The losses remain suspended and carry over to the replacement property activity", "The losses are released and deductible in the year of the exchange because a disposition occurred", "The losses are permanently disallowed because Section 1031 exchanges void prior suspended losses", "The losses convert to a basis adjustment that increases the replacement property''s depreciable basis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 469(g)(1), suspended passive losses are released only upon a fully taxable disposition to an unrelated party. A Section 1031 like-kind exchange is not a fully taxable disposition, so the $55,000 of suspended losses remain suspended and attach to the replacement property. Unlike the second choice suggests, a nontaxable exchange does not trigger the release of suspended passive losses.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: hard / L3 — Ordering of loss limitation rules for limited partner
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Callahan invests $60,000 cash in a limited partnership real estate activity. His 50% share of a $100,000 qualified nonrecourse mortgage is $50,000. In 2026, his allocable share of losses is $130,000. He does not materially participate and has no passive income. His AGI is $80,000. Apply the loss limitation rules in order and determine the deductible amount.', '["$130,000, because qualified nonrecourse financing creates unlimited deductibility for real estate", "$110,000, because basis and at-risk rules apply but passive rules do not limit real estate losses", "$25,000, because the rental loss allowance overrides the at-risk limitation", "$0 in the current year, because Callahan''s limited partner status bars active participation under Section 469(i)(6)(C), and the $110,000 allowed by at-risk rules is fully suspended"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Loss limitations apply in order: (1) basis limits the loss to Callahan''s partnership interest, (2) at-risk rules limit the deductible loss to $110,000 ($60,000 cash plus $50,000 qualified nonrecourse), and (3) passive activity rules suspend the entire $110,000 because Callahan has no passive income and, as a limited partner under Section 469(i)(6)(C), cannot claim the $25,000 active participation allowance. Unlike the second choice suggests, passive rules do restrict real estate losses for non-participating limited partners.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q20: medium / L2 — Qualified nonrecourse financing from a qualified lender
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Robinson invests in a real estate partnership. The partnership obtains a $500,000 loan from a commercial bank, secured solely by the real property, with no personal guarantee from any partner. Robinson''s 20% share of the loan is $100,000. Under Section 465(b)(6), does this financing increase Robinson''s at-risk amount?', '["No, because nonrecourse financing never increases a taxpayer''s at-risk amount for any activity", "No, because the at-risk rules require recourse debt where the taxpayer has personal liability", "Yes, because qualified nonrecourse financing from a qualified lender secured by real property is included in the at-risk amount", "Yes, but only when the borrower also pledges personal assets beyond the real property as collateral"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 465(b)(6), qualified nonrecourse financing is included in a taxpayer''s at-risk amount for real estate activities when the loan is from a qualified lender (such as a bank), is secured by real property used in the activity, and no person is personally liable. Robinson''s $100,000 share qualifies. Unlike the first choice suggests, the qualified nonrecourse financing exception specifically allows certain nonrecourse debt to increase the at-risk amount for real property activities.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q21: hard / L4 — Evaluate converting rental to nonrental via short-term strategy
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'A CPA is advising Torres, who owns a vacation rental generating $50,000 annual losses. Torres has $300,000 of salary income and AGI exceeding $150,000, making the $25,000 rental allowance fully phased out. The current average rental period is 12 days. The CPA proposes shortening the average rental period below 7 days and increasing Torres''s participation to 520 hours to reclassify the activity. Evaluate whether this strategy would convert the losses to deductible nonpassive losses.', '["Yes, because reducing the average rental period below 7 days removes the activity from the rental category, and 520 hours establishes material participation, making losses nonpassive", "No, because the 7-day rule applies only to hotel properties, not vacation rentals", "No, because even if reclassified as nonrental, the losses remain passive unless Torres qualifies as a real estate professional", "Yes, but only $25,000 of losses become deductible under the active participation allowance"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Reg. Section 1.469-1T(e)(3)(ii)(A), activities with an average customer use period of 7 days or less are not rental activities. If Torres achieves this threshold and participates 520 hours (satisfying the 500-hour material participation test), the activity becomes a nonpassive trade or business, making the $50,000 loss fully deductible. Unlike the third choice suggests, real estate professional status is required only for activities that remain classified as rental activities—nonrental activities follow standard participation rules.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q22: hard / L4 — PTP disposition strategy for releasing suspended losses
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'A tax advisor is reviewing Santana''s portfolio, which includes a publicly traded partnership interest with $95,000 of cumulative suspended passive losses. The PTP generates $8,000 of passive income annually. Santana also has $60,000 of passive income from private partnerships. She is considering: (1) hold and absorb losses over 12 years, (2) sell on the market for a $10,000 gain, or (3) gift to charity. Evaluate which strategy maximizes the tax benefit from the suspended losses.', '["Hold, because the private partnership income can absorb PTP losses over time", "Sell, because a fully taxable disposition releases all $95,000 of suspended losses to offset the gain and nonpassive income", "Gift to charity, because the charitable deduction plus released losses yields the greatest total benefit", "Hold, because the $8,000 annual PTP income will absorb losses within 12 years with no capital gain exposure"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 469(g)(1), selling the PTP in a fully taxable disposition releases all $95,000 of suspended losses, which offset the $10,000 gain and generate $85,000 of deductions against nonpassive income. Under Section 469(k), PTP losses can only offset income from the same PTP, so the private partnership income in the first choice cannot absorb PTP losses. Gifting does not trigger loss release because gifts are not fully taxable dispositions.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Extraordinary personal services rental exception
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Wallace owns a nursing care facility where patients stay for an average of 45 days. The facility provides 24-hour skilled nursing, medication management, and rehabilitation services. Employees spend over 60% of their time providing direct patient care. Wallace materially participates. Under the rental activity exceptions, how is this activity classified?', '["Rental activity because the average customer use period exceeds 30 days", "Rental activity eligible for the $25,000 special allowance", "Nonrental activity because extraordinary personal services are provided in connection with making the property available", "Portfolio activity because health care facilities are excluded from passive activity rules"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Reg. Section 1.469-1T(e)(3)(ii)(B), an activity is not a rental activity if extraordinary personal services are provided in connection with making the property available for customer use. Because employees spend over 50% of their time providing personal services (skilled nursing care), this exception applies despite the long average stay. Unlike the first choice suggests, the 30-day average use period test is a separate exception—the extraordinary personal services exception applies regardless of rental period.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: easy / L1 — Taxpayers subject to at-risk rules
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Which taxpayers are subject to the at-risk rules under Section 465?', '["Only partnerships and S corporations at the entity level", "Only individual taxpayers with AGI exceeding $150,000", "Only taxpayers engaged in real estate activities", "Individuals, partners, S corporation shareholders, and closely held C corporations"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 465(a) applies the at-risk rules to individuals and closely held C corporations for all activities. Partners and S corporation shareholders apply the rules at the individual level for their allocable shares of losses. Unlike the first choice suggests, the at-risk rules are not limited to entity-level application—they apply broadly to individuals regardless of the entity form through which the activity is conducted.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — General definition of passive activity
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'What is the general definition of a passive activity under Section 469?', '["Any activity generating less than $25,000 of annual income", "Any trade or business in which the taxpayer does not materially participate, or any rental activity", "Any investment activity that produces portfolio income", "Any activity conducted through a limited liability company"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 469(c) defines a passive activity as any trade or business in which the taxpayer does not materially participate, plus any rental activity (subject to exceptions). This two-part definition captures both non-participatory business activities and rental activities. Unlike the third choice suggests, portfolio income (interest, dividends, royalties) is specifically excluded from the passive activity category under Section 469(e).', 'easy', 1),

-- Q26: hard / L4 — Unintended consequence of grouping election for mixed portfolio
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Ortega is a qualifying real estate professional who owns six rental properties. Four generate losses totaling $200,000; two generate income totaling $80,000. She materially participates in the four loss properties individually but not in the two income properties (40 hours each). She has $50,000 of passive losses from a non-real-estate limited partnership. Her CPA suggests the Section 469(c)(7) grouping election to treat all six as a single activity. Evaluate the unintended consequence.', '["The election would reclassify all rental income as portfolio income", "The election would trigger recapture of prior-year passive losses", "The election has no downside because aggregation always maximizes deductible losses for real estate professionals", "The election would recharacterize the $80,000 of rental income as nonpassive, reducing passive income available to offset the $50,000 non-real-estate passive losses"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Reg. Section 1.469-9(g), the grouping election aggregates all rentals into one activity. Because Ortega materially participates in the aggregate, all income and losses become nonpassive. Without the election, the $80,000 from the non-materially-participated properties would remain passive, available to offset the $50,000 non-real-estate passive losses. Unlike the third choice suggests, the election can convert useful passive income to nonpassive, losing the ability to shelter other passive losses.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Related party lending disqualifies nonrecourse financing
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Fletcher invests in a real estate limited partnership. The partnership borrows $400,000 from Fletcher''s sibling, who holds no interest in the partnership. The loan is nonrecourse, secured by the real property. Fletcher''s 25% share is $100,000. Under Section 465(b)(6), is this loan treated as qualified nonrecourse financing?', '["No, because qualified nonrecourse financing must come from a qualified person who is not related to the taxpayer under Section 465(b)(6)(D)", "Yes, because the loan is nonrecourse and secured by real property", "Yes, because the lender holds no partnership interest", "No, because only banks and government agencies can provide qualified nonrecourse financing"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 465(b)(6)(D) requires qualified nonrecourse financing to come from a qualified person—someone actively engaged in lending who is not related to the taxpayer. Fletcher''s sibling is a related party, disqualifying the loan regardless of other characteristics. The $100,000 does not increase Fletcher''s at-risk amount. Unlike the second choice suggests, being nonrecourse and secured by real property alone is insufficient—the lender must also be a qualified unrelated person.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — At-risk recapture trigger event
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'What event triggers at-risk loss recapture under Section 465(e)?', '["The taxpayer sells the activity at a gain", "The taxpayer contributes additional capital to the activity", "The taxpayer''s at-risk amount is reduced below zero through distributions, debt changes, or loss allocations", "The taxpayer fails to materially participate in the activity for two consecutive years"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 465(e) requires a taxpayer to recapture previously deducted at-risk losses when the at-risk amount drops below zero. This can occur through distributions, changes in debt structure, or conversion of recourse to nonrecourse financing. Unlike the first choice suggests, selling an activity at a gain does not trigger at-risk recapture—it may release suspended losses but does not create a negative at-risk situation.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — Refinancing effect on at-risk amount
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Hammond has $150,000 at risk in a real estate partnership, including $80,000 of cash contributions and $70,000 from a qualified nonrecourse bank mortgage. The partnership refinances the bank loan with a $70,000 seller-financed note from the property''s original seller. How does this affect Hammond''s at-risk amount?', '["No change, because the total debt amount remains $70,000", "The at-risk amount decreases by $70,000 to $80,000, because seller financing from the property''s seller is not qualified nonrecourse financing", "The at-risk amount increases by $70,000 to $220,000, because refinancing creates additional at-risk basis", "The at-risk amount decreases to $0 because any refinancing event resets the calculation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 465(b)(6)(D)(ii), qualified nonrecourse financing cannot come from a person from whom the taxpayer acquired the property. Refinancing from a bank loan (qualified) to seller financing (not qualified) removes $70,000 from Hammond''s at-risk amount, reducing it to $80,000. Unlike the first choice suggests, the identity of the lender matters—even though the loan amount is unchanged, the shift to a disqualified lender reduces the at-risk amount.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — Trust material participation through trustee activities
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'The Wilson Family Trust owns a 100% interest in a landscaping business. The trustee devotes 550 hours annually to managing the business, including hiring employees, negotiating contracts, and overseeing daily operations. Under the passive activity rules, can the trust establish material participation through the trustee''s activities?', '["Yes, because the trustee''s direct participation on behalf of the trust counts toward the 500-hour test under applicable case law and regulations", "No, because trusts are categorically excluded from the material participation rules", "Yes, but only if the trust has at least two individual beneficiaries who also participate", "No, because only the beneficiaries'' personal participation counts toward the trust''s material participation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Reg. Section 1.469-5T and subsequent guidance (including Frank Aragona Trust v. Commissioner), trustees'' direct participation in an activity on behalf of the trust counts toward material participation tests. The trustee''s 550 hours satisfy the 500-hour threshold, making the business nonpassive for the trust. Unlike the fourth choice suggests, it is the trustee''s direct involvement—not beneficiary participation—that establishes material participation for the trust entity.', 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
